{"name":"Nuclear Waste","hex":"#7cfc00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nuclear waste","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The more or less radioactive waste produced by nuclear reactors and others."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nuclear_waste"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radioactive waste","blocks":[{"text":"Radioactive waste is a type of hazardous waste that contains radioactive material. It is a result of various activities, including nuclear medicine, nuclear research, nuclear power generation, nuclear decommissioning, rare-earth mining, and nuclear weapons reprocessing. The storage and disposal of radioactive waste is regulated by government agencies in order to protect human health and the environment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/TINT_Radioactive_wastes%27_barrel.jpg/330px-TINT_Radioactive_wastes%27_barrel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_waste","wikidata":"Q185252"},{"title":"Waste","blocks":[{"text":"Waste are unwanted or unusable materials. Waste is any substance discarded after primary use, or is worthless, defective and of no use. A by-product, by contrast is a joint product of relatively minor economic value. A waste product may become a by-product, joint product or resource through an invention that raises a waste product's value above zero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Shredded_solid_waste.jpg/330px-Shredded_solid_waste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waste","wikidata":"Q45701"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/1280px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAir_pollution3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Activityofuranium233.jpg/500px-Activityofuranium233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Activityofuranium233.jpg/500px-Activityofuranium233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Activity of U-233 for three fuel types. In the case of MOX, the U-233 increases for the first 650 thousand years as it is produced by the decay of Np-237 which was created in the reactor by absorption of neutrons by U-235.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AActivityofuranium233.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Uranium_and_thorium_release_from_coal_combustion.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Uranium_and_thorium_release_from_coal_combustion.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Annual release of uranium and thorium radioisotopes from coal combustion, predicted by ORNL in 1993 to cumulatively amount to 2.9 Mt over the 1937–2040 period, from the combustion of an estimated 637 Gt of coal worldwide.[26]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUranium_and_thorium_release_from_coal_combustion.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Fort-greely-low-level-waste.jpg/500px-Fort-greely-low-level-waste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.